  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Project Scope**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got a handle on the user\'s need: they want a luxurious, animated 3D website for clothes. I\'m focusing on the core requirements now. Finding clothes from different brands and body types is key, as is the interactive 3D preview aspect for an immersive experience.\n\n\n**Exploring Design Options**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how to respond. The user\'s request for design options is leading me to build a high-end conceptual prototype as a demonstration. The core goal is a \"luxurious 3D animated\" website, but I am now figuring out *how* to deliver that concept. I plan to present three design directions internally, then select one for the prototype. I\'m thinking of using React, Tailwind, and Framer Motion.\n\n\n**Refining Implementation Choices**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the tech stack. The user wants \"luxurious 3D animated\", so I\'m moving toward an actual 3D experience with React Three Fiber. I\'m going to install the necessary libraries (`three`, `@react-three/fiber`, and `@react-three/drei`) to create an interactive landing page that feels truly 3D, and the landing page should be the first deliverable. I want the landing page to *feel* more advanced than the initial presentation I had in mind.\n\n\n**Committing to 3D**\n\nThis is exciting! React Three Fiber is the perfect choice for the website\'s luxurious, animated 3D feel, it should blow everyone away. The architecture is taking shape: a luxurious dark theme with an abstract 3D sculpture in a `Canvas`, powered by Drei\'s `MeshDistortMaterial`. Framer Motion will handle text reveals, and I will create an \"Aura - Luxury Fit\" marquee. I\'ll start by installing the necessary dependencies: `three`, `@react-three/fiber`, `@react-three/drei`, `lucide-react`, and `motion`, then start writing the `metadata.json` file.\n\n\n**Building the UI**\n\nI\'m now focusing on UI components, specifically for a 3D scene and overlay. I\'m confirming that `@react-three/fiber` works well with Vite. I am leaning towards building a UI with a 3D background with three theme toggles to directly address the user\'s need for design options, instead of just describing design themes. This is a crucial step towards delivering a compelling prototype.\n\n\n**Designing the Prototype**\n\nI am now solidifying the prototype\'s core features. The plan is to create three distinct themes: \"Noir Elegance,\" \"Silk & Stone,\" and \"Prismatic Glass,\" each with unique 3D spheres. I\'ll mock up key features like \"Discover by Body Type,\" a brand selector, and a \"3D Virtual Try-On\" CTA. I am installing the necessary dependencies: `three`, `@types/three`, `@react-three/fiber`, and `@react-three/drei`. I will update the `metadata.json` file. Next, I\'ll create `Scene.tsx` and `Overlay.tsx` components.\n\n\n**Implementing the UI**\n\nI\'m now drafting code to bring the UI to life, focusing on combining the scene and overlay components. I\'m editing `App.tsx` and `index.css` for overall styling and setup. I\'ve begun coding `Scene.tsx` with a `Canvas`, environment, and a floating sphere using `MeshDistortMaterial`, customizing it based on the theme.\n\n\n**Crafting the Luxury UI**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the finer details of the user interface. I\'m focusing on \"Luxurious typography\". I\'m planning to use `motion.div` elements for fade-ins. I have decided to use serif fonts for luxury, like `Playfair Display`, and a sans-serif like `Inter` or `Montserrat` for the body. I will add the fonts to `index.html` via Google Fonts. I\'m also planning the tool calls sequence: installing packages, editing `index.html` and `metadata.json`, creating `ModelView.tsx` and `LuxuryUI.tsx`, editing `App.tsx`, and compiling. Finally, I\'m reminding myself to use \"curate\", \"discover\", \"experience\", and \"form\" instead of more generic SaaS verbs. I will avoid purple-to-blue gradients and stick to solid luxury tones.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Style**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the design system. I\'ve selected `Cormorant Garamond` and `Jost` for typography, moving away from generic defaults. The minimal container padding is confirmed at 16px. I am drafting `App.tsx` and now understand how to deliver the \"design options\": a dynamic prototype with three distinct themes.\n\n\n**Developing the Themes**\n\nI am now structuring the `ModelView.tsx` component, including the themes, `noir`, `alabaster`, and `prismatic`. I\'m writing the function that selects the right material for each theme. I\'ve also set the background color, ensuring each theme is distinct and appropriately luxurious. I am adding a light component.\n\n\n**Evaluating Material Options**\n\nI\'m now in the thick of choosing the right 3D material. Initially, I considered `MeshDistortMaterial`, but I\'m now exploring if it fully supports transmission and IOR. Turns out, it extends `MeshPhysicalMaterial`, so it should be fine. I\'m testing the limits of `MeshDistortMaterial` and considering other options, such as rendering different materials dynamically if needed.\n\n\n**Defining Font Options**\n\nI\'m now implementing the UI elements. I\'ve updated `index.html` to include the `Cormorant Garamond` and `Jost` fonts. I\'ve also added them to the `index.css` file using Tailwind\'s custom properties. I\'m excited about this new tooling I\'ve found! I\'m moving on to building out other UI elements.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 29
      nanos: 236393733
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
