  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Iconography Options**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the user\'s request to revamp the abstract body type icons. My goal is to replace them with literal representations for improved clarity. The user\'s feedback directs me to visualize the icons as a torso and head. I believe this will create a more direct visual connection.\n\n\n**Analyzing Icon Possibilities**\n\nI\'ve been evaluating `lucide-react` for suitable icons representing various body types, specifically aiming for \"torso and head\" visualizations as per the user\'s direction. My review indicates that the available icons are not anatomically specific. I\'m considering using icons like `User`, `Accessibility`, or `PersonStanding`, but these seem too generic. The `Shirt` icon offers potential, but its direct representation of body type is indirect. This requires a rethink on how to proceed.\n\n\n**Resolving Icon Conflicts**\n\nI\'m now in a tricky situation; I\'m facing a design constraint as I cannot create custom SVG icons. The framework strictly mandates using icons only from `lucide-react`. I searched for human figures like `PersonStanding`, `Accessibility`, and `User` with no luck. I\'ve considered clothing shapes (`Shirt`, `T-Shirt`), but the user specified a torso and head. I am left with few options to fulfill the request. I have to either find a creative workaround or clearly explain this limitation.\n\n\n**Communicating Icon Constraints**\n\nMy focus has shifted to communicating the constraints clearly. I\'ve concluded I have to explain the `lucide-react` limitations regarding custom SVG icons. While searching through `lucide-react`, I came across a few options like `User` and `PersonStanding`, but these are general. I\'m now exploring how to best present the available icons (like `Cylinder`, `Hourglass`, `RectangleHorizontal`, `Ruler`) to meet the intent behind the user\'s request. My plan is to emphasize that the abstract shapes might still be the best fit within the constraints.\n\n\n**Exploring Icon Combinations**\n\nI\'m now investigating combining icons. My current direction is to pair a `User` icon (representing the head and shoulders) with an abstract shape beneath it, which would allow for multiple configurations. I\'m exploring variations on existing `User` icons and also `Accessibility`, and `UserRound`. I am also researching potential tailoring or clothing size icons from `lucide-react` such as `Ruler`, `Scissors`, or `Tape` for inspiration. I still have to avoid creating custom SVGs.\n\n\n**Refining Icon Designs**\n\nI\'m now refining the icon combinations based on available `lucide-react` options. My current thought is to use `User` for slim, `Dumbbell` for athletic (if it\'s there), `UserSquare` for broad, `Hourglass` for curvy, and `UserRound` for round. I\'m re-evaluating whether or not I should stick with abstract shapes like `Hourglass` for each one instead of trying to use `User` variants since these may not be great body-type representations.\n\n\n**Implementing CSS Transformation**\n\nI\'ve hit on a breakthrough: I can use CSS transforms on the `User` icon to achieve the desired body type variations while adhering to the `lucide-react` limitations. I\'m testing `scale-x` to create slim, broad, and athletic figures, and `scale-y` for a curvy figure. This seems promising, but I\'m concerned about the visual distortion of the head. I\'m experimenting to see what works best.\n\n\n**Finalizing Icon Selection**\n\nI\'m now solidifying my icon choices, leaning towards a direct approach within the framework\'s limitations. I\'ll propose `User` for slim, `Dumbbell` or `BicepsFlexed` for athletic, `UserSquare` for broad, `Hourglass` for curvy, and `UserRound` for round. I\'m prioritizing the established metaphors while ensuring that the request for distinct icons is clearly addressed. I\'ll need to clearly explain the constraints on custom SVGs when communicating my suggestions to the user.\n\n\n**Reassessing Icon Options**\n\nI\'m now re-evaluating the icon choices, spurred on by the user\'s direct request for body-type-specific representations, specifically with a torso and head. Given my constraints, I\'m focusing on the closest human-centric icons available. I\'m considering `Ruler` for slim, `Dumbbell` for athletic, `Scaling` or `Square` for broad, `Hourglass` for curvy, and `Circle` for round, however these are all abstract. I need to clearly convey the limitations on creating custom SVGs while still offering suitable alternatives.\n\n\n**Proposing Typography Solution**\n\nI\'m now fully committed to the constraints and have shifted to proposing a solution. After revisiting the request and the limitations imposed, I\'ve decided to suggest that we forego the icons altogether for this step, as `lucide-react` lacks the necessary anatomy-specific icons. Instead, I\'ll emphasize using clear, elegant typography for each body type label. I believe this aligns well with the \"Minimalist/Luxurious\" aesthetic and avoids any \"AI Slop\" from overly simplified SVG attempts. I\'ll need to clearly present this approach and explain the rationale. I\'m double-checking for any suitable `User` variants, just in case.\n\n\n**Suggesting Descriptive Typography**\n\nI\'ve decided to pivot towards a typography-focused solution, acknowledging the constraints with `lucide-react`. I\'ll use the `User` icon for all body types, but pair it with descriptive secondary text. For example, \"Slim / Slender\" would get \"Narrower shoulders, lean build\" below it. This approach elegantly addresses the anatomical request while leveraging clear typography for differentiation, aligning well with the design aesthetic. I\'ve drafted a button component.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 29
      nanos: 183266933
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
