  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Begin Visual Distinction**\n\nI\'m now focusing on visually differentiating body types using icons, as requested. The goal is to move beyond the generic \"User\" icon and create more specific representations. This initial phase involves brainstorming potential visual metaphors for different body types. I\'m exploring how to quickly convey these variations in a recognizable way.\n\n\n**Refine Icon Choices**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `lucide-react` library. The challenge is clear: create distinct icons for athletic, broad, curvy, and round body types without using overly abstract shapes. I\'m focusing on \"torso and head\" representations that can subtly convey these distinctions, given the limitations of the icon set. I\'m considering variations of the `User` icon alongside other promising options like the `Dumbbell` (though initially rejected) or other metaphors for physical traits, to see if they can work.\n\n\n**Explore Icon Metaphors**\n\nI\'m now investigating alternative icons beyond the initial `lucide-react` options. The original attempt, which explored states of `User`-based icons, seems problematic. I\'m exploring the use of clothing items like `Shirt` to represent body types, but now I\'m leaning toward combining icons, or using other metaphors. The `PersonStanding` and `FigureStanding` icons look promising and I\'m reconsidering `Dumbbell`, which I initially rejected. My goal is to find icons that clearly convey the differences in torso and head shapes, as directly requested. I\'m trying to move away from states, and create true visual differences.\n\n\n**Consider Icon Variations**\n\nI\'ve realized that modifying the existing `User` icon via CSS transforms, as initially thought, isn\'t ideal because it distorts stroke widths. My focus now is on finding a way to control stroke width reliably when scaling. I\'m exploring whether the `vector-effect=\"non-scaling-stroke\"` attribute can be applied, or if there\'s a workaround. I\'m also still thinking about combining icons, but I\'m primarily stuck on scaling the `User` icon.\n\n\n**Refine Icon Choices**\n\nI\'m now back to evaluating the available `lucide-react` icons more closely. I\'m looking at combining them to represent diverse body types. I\'m focusing on \"torso and head\" representations, especially the `User` icon as a base, and how I can adjust it. It\'s looking like `User`, `UserRound`, and `UserSquare` might work, in combination, and I\'m also now going to attempt using CSS transforms with `vector-effect=\"non-scaling-stroke\"` to stretch the `User` icon directly.\n\n\n**Constructing Custom SVGs**\n\nI\'m now moving to a new solution, creating custom SVGs for each body type. My intention is to build five unique SVGs that follow the `Lucide` aesthetic, ensuring a 24x24 viewBox, specific stroke widths, and rounded caps and joins. I\'m focusing on crafting distinct visual representations: slim (oval head, narrow shoulders), athletic (V-taper), broad (straight shoulders), curvy (hourglass), and round (rounded shoulders, wider midsection). I\'m planning to implement this by drawing from the base `User` path, and then adapting the shapes, to follow the requirements.\n\n\n**Crafting Athletic SVGs**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the \"athletic\" SVG, focusing on the torso path. I\'m experimenting with different paths to achieve a V-taper. I\'m trying to translate the concept of wide shoulders and a narrower waist into the SVG, using relative and absolute coordinates. I\'m focusing on adjustments, specifically working on a path with wide shoulders that taper down to the bottom. I think I\'m close now!\n\n\n\n**Refining SVG Details**\n\nI\'m now iterating on SVG paths, focusing on making the athletic icon\'s torso a distinct V-shape. I\'ve switched to `C` curves to achieve a smoother, less trapezoidal look. I\'m also now thinking through broader, curvy, and round body types. I\'m focusing on finding the right balance between simplicity and visual clarity, especially with the hourglass curves. Now, I\'m working with simpler shoulder and hip placements.\n\n\n**Implement SVG Scaling**\n\nI\'ve discarded my current SVG math and considered a new solution. Now, I\'m leaning toward a hybrid approach. I will combine standard Lucide icons and scale them using CSS transforms to imply the shape. I\'ll use `stroke-width: 1 / scale` to maintain the 1px stroke. This strategy simplifies things by using an existing base while also providing the control I need to vary proportions. I\'m now writing a `BodyIcon` wrapper component to control the process.\n\n\n**Refine SVG Code**\n\nI\'m now refining the SVG code for each body type, starting with the provided example icons. I\'m focusing on making these SVG\'s luxuriously precise, as requested. I\'m also ensuring the code follows the Lucide aesthetic, with the 24x24 viewBox, and 1px stroke. I\'m focusing on drawing from the `User` icon base, adapting for a sleek and athletic look. I will move to broader shapes.\n\n\n\n**Reviewing Icon Aesthetics**\n\nI\'m now mentally reviewing the SVGs for slim, athletic, broad, curvy, and round icons. Each now incorporates subtle proportional adjustments to reflect the different body types. I\'m checking for visual consistency and clarity across all designs, to avoid confusion.\n\n\n**Construct Precise Torso Shapes**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on defining torso paths with precise coordinates for curvy and round body types. I\'m focusing on crafting smooth curves and rounded shapes for each, using `M`, `C`, and `H` path commands in the SVGs, based on a base torso width of 16. I\'ve mapped a perfect torso structure for curvy, and I\'m drafting a round shape for a wide dome look.\n\n\n"
