  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering User Input**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the user\'s need to replace the existing \"head and torso\" icons with full-body icons for body type selection. I\'m taking a close look at why they feel this change is necessary and the potential implications of this switch. My goal is to completely grasp their request.\n\n\n**Developing Icon Solutions**\n\nI\'m now drafting the SVG paths for the full-body icons. I\'m focusing on creating five distinct silhouettes with a luxury aesthetic, using minimalist geometric strokes and a 1px stroke, to ensure they match the existing `lucide-react` style. The layout includes a head, body, and legs. I am creating slender, athletic, broad, curvy, and round designs. I\'ve begun with the \"slim\" SVG, starting with the head.\n\n\n**Refining Silhouettes**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the slim silhouette. I\'ve been experimenting with simplifying the body and leg shapes, aiming for elegance while using continuous paths. Initially, I was drawing separate lines, but now I\'m using continuous outlines. My latest try is using arcs and lines. I\'m focusing on achieving a fashion croquis aesthetic. I\'ve started the slim silhouette.\n\n\n**Designing Icon Structures**\n\nI\'ve simplified the body icons to continuous paths, outlining the silhouettes. I\'ve completed the \"slim,\" \"athletic,\" \"broad,\" and \"curvy\" icons with minimalist designs. Each starts with a circle head, and the body uses a continuous stroke. I\'m aiming for elegant silhouettes. I am now working on a \"round\" O-shape icon.\n\n\n**Optimizing Visuals**\n\nI\'m now refining the SVG path for the \"slim\" figure, aiming for a polished look. I\'ve moved past rigid outlines and am trying to create a full body outline. The continuous path for the new slim figure is using elegant curves. I\'m focusing on ensuring it clearly represents body type. My latest path uses elegant curves to form a better visual.\n\n\n**Constructing Visuals**\n\nI\'m now implementing a new body outline with volume. I\'m focusing on the \"slim\" SVG and have added a curved path from left shoulder to right shoulder, then down to the right ankle, across the feet, to left ankle and back to left waist. The updated code will ensure it clearly represents body shape.\n\n\n**Crafting SVG Elements**\n\nI\'m now diving into the SVG code for the \"curvy\" icon, adjusting the path to refine the outline. I\'m moving away from `C` curves and favoring `Q` or `L` segments to simplify the code while maintaining a natural flow. The goal is a clean, visually appealing \"hourglass\" silhouette. I\'ve been fine-tuning the curves to best represent this.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing Icon Geometry**\n\nI\'m now focused on refining the SVG paths for the \"round\" icon, paying close attention to its legs. I\'m reviewing and adjusting the curves to ensure a soft, wide silhouette that accurately represents the fuller figure. The paths are taking shape.\n\n\n\n**Refining Coordinates**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on optimizing the coordinates for the \"slim,\" \"athletic,\" and \"broad\" icons, fine-tuning their silhouettes. I\'ve re-evaluated X coordinates, updating the positions for the shoulder, waist and ankles of each. My goal is precision. I\'m focusing on crafting a balanced, minimalist look for each.\n\n\n**Adjusting Silhouettes**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the SVG paths, meticulously refining each body type for visual accuracy. I\'ve been optimizing coordinates, focusing on the \"slim,\" \"athletic,\" and \"broad\" icons, while taking detailed notes on the shapes. I am particularly focusing on the leg separation of the \"curvy\" and \"round\" designs.\n\n\n**Evaluating Icon Refinement**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the leg and neck curves for all body icons. I\'m focusing on ensuring they are fashion-forward and elegant by using smooth curves, as well as refining neck/head connections. I\'m prioritizing the visual appeal of these designs. I\'m also preparing for a quick code update to `/src/components/VirtualFitting.tsx`.\n\n\n**Implementing Icon Updates**\n\nI\'ve just added three new body icons: \"athletic\", \"broad\", and \"curvy\". I\'ve created separate React components for each, using `svg` elements and paths. Each SVG icon contains a circle head and path for the body, and are all fully functional.\n\n\n**Reviewing Visual Implementations**\n\nI\'m now implementing the new \"round\" and \"slim\" SVG icons in `/src/components/VirtualFitting.tsx`, and ensuring they function correctly with the other icons. I\'ve focused on ensuring the visual height distribution is correct, and I\'ve also incorporated a uniform `strokeWidth`. I\'m checking overall consistency.\n\n\n**Optimizing Curve Geometry**\n\nI\'m now refining the SVG path for the \"curvy\" icon, and I\'ve replaced the sharp waist point with an elegant hourglass curve using `Q` segments. I\'ve also switched to `Q` for smooth curves in \"athletic\" and \"slim\" icons as well. I\'m focusing on consistency across body types, by crafting elegant curves.\n\n\n**Verifying Code Implementation**\n\nI\'m now implementing and reviewing the \"slim\" SVG, using elegant curves within the React component. I am examining the rendered output. I\'ve focused on ensuring the path uses `Q` segments for smooth transitions. The overall goal is visual refinement and a polished outcome.\n\n\n\n**Finalizing Body Types**\n\nI am now focusing on the final two body types, \"round\" and \"slim\", integrating the elegant curves previously established. I\'m taking the previous components, and optimizing them. I\'m ensuring both icons, and the overall design system, are consistent. I\'m nearly done!\n\n\n"
