    thinking: "**Interpreting the Question**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the core of the user\'s question, understanding they\'re puzzled by the `Gem` icon used for \"Womenswear\". I\'m interpreting their implicit question as seeking the rationale behind this design choice. I\'m focusing on why a gem, rather than another symbol, represents that particular category. I\'m now exploring possible semantic links between gems and women\'s clothing, as well as considering design principles.\n\n\n**Analyzing Alternatives**\n\nI see a clear problem: the current icon assignment is inconsistent. The user\'s implicit issue is the `Gem` icon\'s suitability for \"Womenswear\" when compared to \"Menswear\" and \"Gender Neutral.\" I\'m looking at potential solutions, from using a different `lucide-react` icon to crafting custom SVGs. Currently, I\'m considering using `User` for all categories, custom SVGs for gendered categories, or clothing-related icons.\n\n\n**Refining Icon Concepts**\n\nI\'m now iterating on specific icon ideas. Creating custom SVGs, I can address the gendered icon issue. I\'m imagining minimalist SVG figures: male, female, and neutral representations to match the body type icons. I\'m weighing options for Menswear \342\200\223 a suit, shirt, or croquis? For Womenswear \342\200\223 a dress, croquis, or even a hanger? The goal is continuous line art for each category.\n\n\n**Developing Icon Prototypes**\n\nI\'m now iterating through specific icon concepts. I\'ve decided on custom SVGs using continuous line art. For menswear, a sharp-shouldered silhouette. For womenswear, a tapered waist/curved silhouette. I\'m exploring an abstract form for gender-neutral categories, leaning towards body type icons. I\'m referencing the body type icons for inspiration. Alternatively, I may use Lucide icons for these categories.\n\n\n**Crafting Icon Designs**\n\nI\'ve moved beyond conceptualization to actual design. I\'m focusing on creating clean, geometric SVGs. I\'ve drafted an initial blazer/shirt outline for Menswear and a dress outline for Womenswear. The womenswear SVG needs refinement, but I have a candidate and I\'m focused on continuous lines for each. I\'m also now considering an oversized shirt or hanger for the gender-neutral category.\n\n\n**Designing the Icons**\n\nI\'ve discarded the gender symbol idea. I\'m now crafting abstract figures using continuous line art, building custom SVGs. I have initial outlines for menswear and womenswear, with more work for the latter. The neutral icon is proving tricky, considering abstraction versus user familiarity. I\'m aiming for elegance and clarity within the design.\n\n\n\n"
