  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining the Concept**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the user\'s dissatisfaction with the \"Womenswear\" icon. They desire a better visual representation of a female figure. I\'m taking the feedback seriously and exploring fresh design approaches. The user specifically indicated the second category button in the selection as the problem area.\n\n\n**Investigating Icon Options**\n\nI\'ve hit a roadblock with custom SVGs, they just aren\'t hitting the mark. I\'m pivoting to explore standard icon options. Currently, I\'m thinking of trying the Venus symbol (\342\231\200), a dress icon, or if it exists in the Lucide set, a female silhouette. The goal is a clean, universally understood representation. I\'m focusing on simplicity and clarity within the 1px-stroke line-art aesthetic and user expectations, as custom options have fallen short.\n\n\n\n**Exploring Icon Solutions**\n\nI\'m now digging into Lucide for potential solutions, checking icons like \"Shirt\" and \"ShoppingBag\" for menswear and shopping concepts, but so far no luck. I\'m expanding the search to include Lucide\'s \"PersonStanding\", and I\'ll keep the Venus symbol in mind. I think I\'ll try making a mathematically perfect custom SVG for a dress icon. I\'m also considering the \"Ribbon\" icon or a clean face profile instead. My current focus is a universally recognizable dress icon as the best choice.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Female Depiction**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the core feedback: the icon needs to clearly depict a female. My previous attempts at abstract representations failed to resonate. Now, I\'m leaning toward a clean, standard female symbol or a simple silhouette. I\'ve re-evaluated the user\'s specific request for better visual representation of a female figure, and now I\'m considering a minimalist side-profile of a woman\'s face, or a refined user avatar style, that would serve the user best.\n\n\n**Designing the Icon**\n\nI\'ve been playing with modifications to the existing \"User\" icon. I\'m focusing on adding a flared dress shape to the design. My previous attempt was jagged, so I\'m now refining the A-line silhouette with smoother curves and adjusted points. I am trying to achieve a clean and elegant female avatar.\n\n\n**Implementing the Icon**\n\nI\'ve decided to move forward with a \"Dress\" icon, and I\'m currently crafting it from scratch. I\'m focusing on simplicity and clean lines to ensure the best possible representation, and trying to avoid any open bottom issues, using a continuous line-based construction. I\'m using the Lucide \"Shirt\" icon to take ideas from. I am also planning on working on a female silhouette icon, an A-line dress avatar.\n\n\n**Designing the Icon**\n\nI\'ve been iterating on a female silhouette and incorporated a few new design elements. I realized a triangle-shape was insufficient. Now, I\'m trying out the A-line dress with curved shoulders to create a universally recognizable female form. I am now refining a more elegant design.\n\n\n**Constructing the SVG**\n\nI\'m now generating the SVG code directly. I\'ve mapped out the coordinates for an A-line dress, aiming for a clean, recognizable female form. I\'ve decided against adding arm definition to maintain its iconic simplicity. I\'m focusing on creating an SVG in the style of the Lucide user icon. My current focus is to combine the existing \"User\" icon structure with a female hair/A-line shape. I\'ve decided to test the `M12 10 C8.5 10 6.5 12.5 6 16 L4.5 22 H19.5 L18 16 C17.5 12.5 15.5 10 12 10 Z` SVG path, which I will combine with the basic Female icon. I am now trying to merge these designs to achieve a premium look.\n\n\n**Evaluating Icon Cohesion**\n\nI\'m now contemplating whether the Venus symbol (\342\231\200) is the correct fit, even though it\'s elegant and globally recognized. My concern is icon cohesion with \"User\" (Menswear) and \"Users\" (Gender Neutral). If \"Womenswear\" is a Venus symbol, it clashes with \"User\". I\'m now exploring the idea of changing all three icons to ensure consistency, or perhaps using a \"UserFemale\" variant for the womenswear category. I am leaning towards \"UserFemale,\" which depicts long hair to distinguish it from the standard \"User\" icon.\n\n\n\n**Refining Hair and Form**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the female user icon. Adding long hair to the standard user icon felt odd, so I\'ve pivoted to a clean woman\'s face profile. The initial A-line dress icon is now back on the table. My focus is on creating a clear portrait within the existing style framework. I\'ll stick to a simple silhouette.\n\n\n**Testing Icon Aesthetics**\n\nI\'m now testing a curved A-line dress icon alongside the \"User\" icon for menswear. The current SVG code incorporates a 3.5 radius circle at (12,6) as the head, with the dress beginning at y=10.5. I\'ve added a curve from (10, 10.5) to (14, 10.5), which then connects down to (18.5, 21) and (5.5, 21). This silhouette feels very fitting and standard. I am assessing the visual compatibility with the existing \"User\" icon.\n\n\n**Finalizing the SVG**\n\nI\'m now generating the final SVG code for the \"Womenswear\" icon. I\'ve opted for a refined A-line silhouette with a distinct head, ensuring a clear and elegant representation. The SVG utilizes a simple circle for the head and a smoothly curved path for the dress, resulting in a design that\'s both recognizable and aesthetically pleasing. I\'m prioritizing cleanliness to blend with existing icons.\n\n\n\n**Constructing the Icon**\n\nI\'m now implementing the final version. I\'m taking the previous ideas and merging them into the final SVG. I am finalizing the iconic dress avatar design and testing the SVG, focusing on a clean appearance. I am using `circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"7\" r=\"4\"` for the head and `path d=\"M 10 11.5 C 7.5 12.5 6 16 5 21 A 1 1 0 0 0 6 22 H 18 A 1 1 0 0 0 19 21\"` for the dress.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Visual**\n\nI\'m now generating the SVG code. I\'m taking the previous ideas and merging them into the final SVG. I am finalizing the iconic dress avatar design and testing the SVG, focusing on a clean appearance. I am using `circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"7\" r=\"4\"` for the head and `path d=\"M 9.5 11 L 5 21 A 1 1 0 0 0 6 22 H 18 A 1 1 0 0 0 19 21 L 14.5 11\"`. The new design is a standard A-line dress, with a circle as the head. This combines perfectly the standard Lucide design language with the concept for the user.\n\n\n**Refining Dress Silhouette**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the final SVG and realizing that ending the dress shape with `Z` will overlap the head. I\'m analyzing the circle\'s equation and calculating the exact point on the bottom of the circle, to ensure there\'s no overlapping. I\'m also ensuring the bottom doesn\'t touch the head circle.\n\n\n"
