    thinking: "**Reviewing the Images**\n\nI\'m now analyzing the user\'s request. It seems they preferred the previous Unsplash backgrounds. I\'m focusing on why, and if they desire better, more fashion-oriented imagery. I\'m also considering their interest in a larger, continuously updating photo gallery. I\'m looking into how best to achieve that goal now.\n\n\n**Refining the Image Solution**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into image sourcing. I will restore and improve the background gallery with high-quality fashion images, possibly using a hardcoded list of reliable Unsplash URLs. Also, I\'m updating the `CLOTHING_DATABASE` to display specific images matching the product descriptions: shirts, pants, shoes, and so on. My focus is on sourcing Unsplash IDs or similar that accurately depict these garments.\n\n\n**Implementing Image Changes**\n\nI\'m now implementing the changes to the image handling. I\'ve separated the background gallery and plan to populate it with a dedicated array of high-quality fashion images. I\'m focusing on ensuring this gallery is distinct from the product images. Also, I\'m working to replace the placeholder images for products in the `CLOTHING_DATABASE` with more accurate and specific visuals. I am using known Unsplash image IDs.\n\n\n**Validating Unsplash IDs**\n\nI\'m now focusing on Unsplash image URLs. I\'ve confirmed that the deprecated source URLs are unreliable, but standard image URLs work with valid IDs. I\'m prioritizing finding and validating these IDs for product representation. I\'ll use known Unsplash fashion photo IDs for the background, and am trying the `curl` commands to confirm functionality. I\'m exploring methods to retrieve valid, reliable IDs for both product and background images.\n\n\n**Verifying Image URLs**\n\nI\'m now focused on validating the URLs for fashion images. I\'ve compiled a list of potential Unsplash IDs for products like shoes, denim jackets, and shirts. I\'m verifying these IDs using a quick script or `curl` loop to ensure they are valid and return the expected images. I\'m exploring reverting to previous URLs from the old version, which the user explicitly preferred. I\'m now testing these older URLs to confirm the fix.\n\n\n**Validating Image IDs**\n\nI\'m now methodically verifying the Unsplash image IDs. I\'ve tested a list of previous IDs and found many are returning 404 errors. I\'ve identified that some IDs appear to be artificially generated, containing timestamps. My new focus is retrieving authentic Unsplash IDs to ensure accurate product representation. To find valid IDs, I\'m now exploring methods to search the Unsplash API via `curl`.\n\n\n**Exploring API Options**\n\nI\'m now investigating alternative image sources. I\'ve discovered the `api.unsplash.com` requires an access key, so I\'m pivoting. I\'m considering using the Pexels API (potentially requiring a key) or an open-source image provider. I could also search for valid Unsplash IDs using DuckDuckGo or a web search. Furthermore, I\'ll attempt a Node script to fetch images from a public API, or consider scraping Unsplash HTML. I\'m going to grab Unsplash IDs from `unsplash.com` homepage or search. Previously, I had issues with an earlier `curl` command, let me retry a different search.\n\n\n**Searching for Images**\n\nI\'m now investigating alternative image sources. The Unsplash API is protected. I\'m considering querying the web using `search_web` for \"site:unsplash.com/photos/ shirt\" or using Pexels or Wikimedia Commons. Picsum.photos is an option, but mostly landscapes. I\'ve discovered the Fake Store API (`https://fakestoreapi.com/products`), which provides 20 real product images, and I\'m running a command to see them. I can use curated Unsplash IDs or a web search for the background gallery.\n\n\n"
