  planner_response {
    response: "I apologize for that visual mix-up! \n\nI investigated the database and found the root cause: a handful of unassigned image IDs were accidentally duplicated across categories. For example, the men\'s **\"Oversized Linen Shirt\"** and **\"Utility Cargo Pants\"** were sharing the exact same internal image URLs as the women\'s **\"Mbroidered Net Saree\"** and **\"Block Print Co-ord Set\"**. Because of this, the application was mistakenly rendering the feminine outfits (including the Saree and the handbag/purse) under the Menswear section.\n\nI have executed a data-level fix to resolve this:\n- **Separated Image References**: I completely replaced the duplicate image URLs for the conflicting men\'s items. \n- **Strict Gender Accuracy**: The Oversized Linen Shirt, Cargo Pants, and Cotton Dobby Kurta now point to distinct, gender-accurate photographs (sourced from Wikimedia Commons). \n- **Isolated Databases**: Women\'s items like the Saree and Pleated Skirts now exclusively retain their original images and will no longer bleed into the male catalog.\n\nIf you refresh or flip back to the Virtual Fitting module, the Menswear section will now strictly display the correct male clothing items without any purses or sarees!"
    modified_response: "I apologize for that visual mix-up! \n\nI investigated the database and found the root cause: a handful of unassigned image IDs were accidentally duplicated across categories. For example, the men\'s **\"Oversized Linen Shirt\"** and **\"Utility Cargo Pants\"** were sharing the exact same internal image URLs as the women\'s **\"Mbroidered Net Saree\"** and **\"Block Print Co-ord Set\"**. Because of this, the application was mistakenly rendering the feminine outfits (including the Saree and the handbag/purse) under the Menswear section.\n\nI have executed a data-level fix to resolve this:\n- **Separated Image References**: I completely replaced the duplicate image URLs for the conflicting men\'s items. \n- **Strict Gender Accuracy**: The Oversized Linen Shirt, Cargo Pants, and Cotton Dobby Kurta now point to distinct, gender-accurate photographs (sourced from Wikimedia Commons). \n- **Isolated Databases**: Women\'s items like the Saree and Pleated Skirts now exclusively retain their original images and will no longer bleed into the male catalog.\n\nIf you refresh or flip back to the Virtual Fitting module, the Menswear section will now strictly display the correct male clothing items without any purses or sarees!"
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
